#{[17592186241023 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,-2,-1⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-4,5,-1⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨1,1,4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186241023 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-4t &=1 \\\\ -2s+5t &=1 \\\\ -s-t &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-4t &=1 \\\\ t &=-1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-4t & =1 \\\\ s-4(-1) & =1 \\\\ s+4 & =1 \\\\ s & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-3,\\,t=-1,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=-3\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-3)\\cdot (-1)=3.$"] [17592186241024 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,-1,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,4,-3⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,-7,3⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186241024 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=3 \\\\ -s+4t &=-7 \\\\ 3s-3t &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-2t &=3 \\\\ t &=-2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-2t & =3 \\\\ s-2(-2) & =3 \\\\ s+4 & =3 \\\\ s & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-1,\\,t=-2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=-\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-1)\\cdot (-2)=2.$"] [17592186241025 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,-1,4⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-3,-8,14⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨5,-10,-14⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186241025 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s-3t &=5 \\\\ -s-8t &=-10 \\\\ 4s+14t &=-14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -8 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -15 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s-3t &=5 \\\\ t &=3 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s-3t & =5 \\\\ -s-3(3) & =5 \\\\ -s-9 & =5 \\\\ s & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-14,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=-14\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-14)\\cdot 3=-42.$"] [17592186314972 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186314972 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"] [17592186315058 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186315058 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} &=-2 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 31\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=31,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}.$"] [17592186315059 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186315059 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 18 & -21 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-18{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+6(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+(-3){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+(-1){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot (-1)=6.$"] [17592186317208 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\mathrm{Π}:\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},$ $t,s\\in \\mathbb{R}$, where  $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,7,-3⟩$, $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-8,10,-4⟩$, and  $\\mathbf{u}=⟨6,9,-4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{u}=s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c}.$"] [17592186317208 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u}\\in \\mathrm{Π}$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{u} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t &=6 \\\\ 7s+10t &=9 \\\\ -3s-4t &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{66}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+28{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s-8t &=6 \\\\ t &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-8t & =6 \\\\ s-8\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =6 \\\\ s+4 & =6 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=2\\mathbf{b}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1.$"] [17592186317213 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186317213 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=6 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+4{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+(-2){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{3}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$"] [17592186342223 :question/problem "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$, and $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}.$\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186342223 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=8.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+(-2){\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=8\\cdot (-2)=-16.$"] [17592186342228 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342228 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & k-6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-15\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-15.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-15\\,⇒\\,k=15.\n$$"] [17592186342229 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342229 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k+12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-3.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-3\\,⇒\\,k=3.\n$$"] [17592186342230 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342230 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\nk=0.\n$$"] [17592186342231 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342231 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=-8 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 9 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 10 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 10 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k+6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-18\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-18.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-18\\,⇒\\,k=18.\n$$"] [17592186342237 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342237 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -32 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-18\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$"] [17592186342238 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186342238 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}},{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{\\mathbf{2}} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 10 & -12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -8 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -30 \\\\ 10 & -12 & k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -30 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-45\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-2 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -30 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+45\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+45.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+45\\,⟹\\,k=-45.\n$$"]}
